---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/site";

const title = "Privacy Policy — yamete";
const description =
  "yamete does not collect or transmit your data. Detection and configuration stay on your Mac.";
const issues = `${REPO}/issues`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="doc">
    <article class="wrap doc__article">
      <a class="back mono" href="/">← Back to yamete</a>

      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="meta mono">Effective date: 8 August 2026</p>

      <section>
        <h2>In one sentence</h2>
        <p>
          yamete does not collect, transmit, or share your data with Chipcolate. Detection
          runs on your Mac; configuration and logs stay under your home directory unless
          <em>you</em> configure an action that sends something elsewhere.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What data we collect</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>None.</strong> There is no account, no sign-up, no analytics SDK, no crash
          reporter that phones home, and no Chipcolate server the app talks to. We do not
          receive sensor samples, slap events, your config, or device identifiers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What the app stores on your Mac</h2>
        <p>Everything lives in standard user-owned locations:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Config</strong> —
            <code>~/Library/Application Support/com.chipcolate.yamete/config.json</code>
            (detection settings, actions you define). Written with owner-only permissions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Control socket</strong> —
            <code>…/yamete.sock</code> for the menu bar app and CLI to talk to the daemon.
            Owner-only; peers are checked by user id.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Logs</strong> —
            <code>~/Library/Logs/yamete/</code> for diagnostics (for example a sound that
            failed to decode). Failed webhooks are logged without query strings so tokens in
            URLs are not written to disk.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Uninstalling the LaunchAgent, quitting the app-owned daemon, and deleting those
          paths removes the data. We never receive a copy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Permissions we request</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> — required by macOS for any process that opens
            HID devices, including the motion sensor yamete reads. It is
            <em>not</em> used to observe keyboards, trackpads as text input, or screen
            content. The daemon opens specific SPU accelerometer and gyroscope devices only;
            it does not seize them from the system.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          No camera, microphone, contacts, photos, full-disk, or accessibility permission is
          required for core detection.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What the sensor path processes</h2>
        <p>
          Acceleration and rotation samples are read in real time for slap detection. They
          are not uploaded. Optional <strong>telemetry</strong> on the local socket exists
          only so the menu bar app can draw the live scope while that window is visible; it
          is reference-counted and off when nobody is watching. Samples are not retained as
          a history except whatever you deliberately record with the CLI tuning tools
          (<code>yamete calibrate</code> / <code>record-suite</code>), which write files you
          choose on disk.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Actions you configure</h2>
        <p>
          yamete can play sounds, run a program you specify, or send an HTTP request you
          configure (webhooks). Those features only do what you set up:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Sound</strong> — opens audio files or folders you pick; no network.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Exec</strong> — starts a program you name, optionally with the slap
            event on stdin and as <code>YAMETE_*</code> environment variables. That process
            is under your control; we do not receive its output.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Webhook</strong> — sends a request to a URL you provide. That is the
            only intentional network path in normal use, and it goes to endpoints
            <em>you</em> choose (Home Assistant, IFTTT, your server, etc.), not to
            Chipcolate.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If a webhook or script includes personal data, that is your configuration. Treat
          webhook URLs and headers as secrets; keep them out of shared screenshots of
          config.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>This website</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://yamete.app">yamete.app</a> is a static site. We do not run
          first-party analytics or advertising on it. Hosting (for example GitHub Pages)
          and your browser may process technical data such as IP addresses under those
          providers’ policies; that traffic does not include sensor data from the app.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Third parties</h2>
        <p>
          The app contains no advertising networks, analytics SDKs, or crash-reporting
          services that contact third parties on our behalf. Optional network use is limited
          to user-configured webhooks and to whatever you run via exec.
        </p>
        <p>
          yamete is not distributed through the Mac App Store. Builds are signed and
          notarised for Gatekeeper, then published as GitHub Releases. Apple’s notarisation
          service sees the binary at submit time under Apple’s terms; Chipcolate does not
          receive usage data from that process. Source and downloads live on GitHub under
          GitHub’s terms.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Children’s privacy</h2>
        <p>
          yamete does not knowingly collect information from anyone — including children
          under 13 (or the equivalent minimum age in your jurisdiction). Because we collect
          no personal data, there is nothing for Chipcolate to delete or export on request.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Your rights (GDPR, CCPA, and similar)</h2>
        <p>
          Those regimes give you rights over personal data held about you. Chipcolate holds
          no personal data about yamete users from the product itself, so there is nothing
          for us to provide or erase. Data on your Mac is under your control — edit the
          config, clear logs, or uninstall to remove it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes, we will update the effective date above and note the
          change in release notes when the product behaviour changes. Because the approach
          is “collect nothing,” material revisions would only accompany a significant
          redesign (for example adding an account or cloud service).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="questions">
        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about this policy go to the project’s issue tracker:
          <a href={issues}>github.com/chipcolate/yamete/issues</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="controller mono">
          CHIPCOLATE SRL · Product: yamete · Apache-2.0
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .doc {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 5rem) var(--rhythm);
    min-height: 100vh;
  }

  .doc__article {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .meta {
    margin: 0 0 3rem;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.55rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  section p,
  section li {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  section p {
    max-width: none;
  }

  section ul {
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
  }

  section li::marker {
    color: var(--blush-ink);
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.82em;
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  .controller {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
